>> And it also illustrates implicit support for wishing for quest
>> artifacts; if only a Tourist could obtain the PYEC, then there would be
>> no need to add any code restricting blessed charging to only Tourists.
> 
> Not so. Merely disabling quest artifact wishing wouldn't mean that
> non-tourist wouldn't get the PYEC ever. Bones-files can have quest
> artifacts.
> 
> I do support prohibiting quest artifact wishing but I would retain the
> possibility to get one from bones file.

Rendering bones files even more of a potentially positive impact on
gameplay than they are already.  If you're going to block non-original
roles from obtaining Quest artifacts, block them completely or don't do
it at all.  I support "don't do it at all", obviously, as a means to
retain variety in the game; but certainly some of the more powerful
Quest artifacts could stand significant tone-down.

> I actually coded a patch that created a powerful playercharcter in
>posession of the wished artifact if one was wished succesfully. You
>might want to check the patch database or Jukka's pages. Some suggested
>that the true owner of the artifact should be brought forth. I might
>code that someday.

The patch is amusing but effectively renders wishing for artifacts moot;
whether you're not able to wish for one because the code says
"forbidden", or you're not able to wish for one because something will
kill you immediately, there's no actual difference to the player, and
this isn't good.  (In other words, by the time you're able to wish for
one, you probably no longer need to.)

Shopkeepers are an excellent example of more careful balance; a
higher-level player can step right up and melee one toe-to-toe, but the
player doesn't have to be so high-level that they no longer need the
gold and toys in the shop.

